          {
            "citations": null,
            "text": "<done>\nI checked all messages in **#product-launch** (C1), **#partner-integrations** (C2), and **#customer-success** (C3) and inspected the reactions on each one via `reactions.get`. Here's what I found:\n\n**#product-launch**\n- \"Launch checklist finalized...\" → :thumbsup: (U2)\n- \"Great work team, shipping the beta today!\" → :tada: (U4)\n- \"Rollback plan attached...\" → :eyes: (U4)\n\n**#partner-integrations**\n- \"Partner API docs updated...\" → :heart: (U1)\n- \"New partner onboarding flow live now.\" → :raised_hands: (U3)\n- \"Integration testing complete...\" → :thumbsup: (U5)\n\n**#customer-success**\n- \"Customer feedback from launch week...\" → :tada: (U3)\n- \"Support macros updated...\" → :raised_hands: (U6)\n- \"Retro notes: launch went smoothly overall.\" → :heart: (U2, U7)\n\n**Result:** No message in any of these three channels has a reaction literally named **'party'**. The closest matching celebratory reaction used is **:tada:** (🎉), left by **U4** (on the product-launch beta announcement) and **U3** (on the customer-success feedback post) — but there is no exact 'party' emoji reaction anywhere in these channels.\n\nSo, for your launch retro shout-out list based on a strict 'party' emoji match: **no reviewers qualify** — nobody used that specific emoji. If :tada: counts as a substitute for \"party,\" the shout-out list would be **U4** and **U3**.",
